{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-103977",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+y \\\\ 2-y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${x}^{3}+y=u$ and $y=2-v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{3}+y)^{2}+y=3\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}+2-v=3\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=2-v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2\\,⟹\\,2-v=2\\,⟹\\,v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}-1 \\\\ v=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 1 \\\\ v=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14166956/assets/60d8d3e30eba31683d1b6bb9bf399aa20f2bda37be1cb31bec431a4e27c0f54e.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{2}-1\\le v\\le 0\\}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
